Regulatory references (normative texts, not code)
-------------------------------------------------

The following legal instruments are referenced by the implementation. They are
reproduced here by reference only; no text of these instruments is included in
this repository and none of them is a dependency of the code.

  * Regulation (EU) 2019/2144 of the European Parliament and of the Council of
    16 December 2019 on general safety of vehicles, and in particular Article 6
    and the provisions requiring an Event Data Recorder on new vehicle types.
    This regulation is the legal basis for the EDR mandate in the European
    Union.

  * UN Regulation No. 160 — "Uniform provisions for the approval of vehicles
    with regard to the Event Data Recorder". Referenced for the data elements
    of Annex 4 (Class A), the trigger thresholds, the definition of T0, the
    locking rules, the data storage capacity and the "complete file"
    indication.

  * Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/545 of 26 April 2022
    supplementing Regulation (EU) 2019/2144 with regard to the Event Data
    Recorder, in particular as regards the requirements for accessing and
    reading the stored data.

  * 49 CFR Part 563 (United States) — Event Data Recording, the North American
    data-element and performance profile referenced for comparison purposes.

The implementation is *aligned with* UN Regulation No. 160. It is not
certified, not homologated and does not claim regulatory compliance.


Data formats and specifications
-------------------------------

  * MCAP — the MCAP specification and the reference C++ library published at
    mcap.dev (Foxglove). Used for the archive format of sealed events and the
    Foxglove visualization layout. Licensed upstream under the Apache License,
    Version 2.0; the licence as published upstream governs any use of that
    code. The MCAP writer and reader shipped in host/mcap/ are an independent
    implementation of the published specification.

  * CRC-32C (Castagnoli) — the check value for the variant used by the journal
    format is CRC32C("123456789") = 0xE3069283. The polynomial and the
    initial/final values are documented in docs/FORMAT.md.


Inspiration (ideas only, no code copied)
----------------------------------------

  * CrashMonkey — research work on testing file-system consistency and crash
    behaviour by controlled crash injection.
  * ALICE — research work on fault injection in storage stacks.
  * dm-log-writes — work on the correctness of log-structured writes under
    crash.

These are cited as inspiration for the *method* used in Ambre (crash
injection, exhaustive exploration of crash states, invariant checking after
recovery). No code, no text and no data from these projects are reproduced
here. They are cited from memory and have not been re-verified against the
current published versions.


Datasets (to be used, not yet redistributed)
--------------------------------------------

  * comma2k19 (comma.ai / comma.ai "comma2k19" driving dataset) — real driving
    data used to derive realistic vehicle signal traces for scenario
    generation. Reported licence: MIT. **To be confirmed** before any part of
    it is downloaded, vendored or redistributed.

  * NHTSA crash test data — public crash-test datasets used to cross-check the
    trigger arithmetic against documented real crash profiles. **Licence to be
    confirmed** before use.

Status: no third-party dataset is currently vendored in this repository. The
scenario files under host/scenarios/ are generated synthetically by the
repository's own generator. The licences of the two datasets above must be
confirmed, and the corresponding attributions added here, before any of their
content is included in a public release or in a demo video.
